#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:221
msgid "Generator expressions: `sum(... for ...)`"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:223
msgid "When you only need the **total**, you do not need the list. Write the comprehension without the square brackets, inside the call:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:238
msgid "This is a **generator expression**: it gives the values one by one to `sum`, and never builds a list in memory. It works with any function that takes many values: `sum`, `min`, `max`, `any`, `all`, `sorted`, `set`, `\", \".join(...)`."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:242
msgid "`sum(1 for ... if ...)` is `COUNT(*) ... WHERE ...`. Another way to count: `True` is `1` (session 2), so `sum(int(row[9]) > 0 for row in rows)` gives the same."
