#. heading 2
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:338
msgid "2. Checks and extremes: `any`, `all`, `min`, `max`"
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:340
msgid "`any` and `all`: questions about the whole list"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:342
msgid "`any(values)` is `True` if **at least one** value is true."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:343
msgid "`all(values)` is `True` if **every** value is true."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:345
msgid "With a generator expression, they ask a question about every row - the checks you run on data before a report:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:359
msgid "In SQL these are `EXISTS (SELECT ... WHERE ...)` and `NOT EXISTS (... WHERE NOT ...)`. Both stop as soon as the answer is known: `any` at the first true value, `all` at the first false one."
msgstr ""

#. box title
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:363
msgid "Empty lists"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:364
msgid "`any([])` is `False` - nothing is true. `all([])` is `True` - nothing is false. A check with `all` on an empty file \"passes\". Check the number of rows too."
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:372
msgid "`min` and `max` with `key`"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:374
msgid "Session 4 showed `key=` with `sorted`, `min` and `max`. With rows, `key` says which column to compare - and you get back the **whole row**, not only the value:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:388
msgid "Without `key`, `max(rows)` compares the rows as lists of strings - the first column first. That is the largest **id**, not the largest loan. And `max` of the column alone gives only the number:"
msgstr ""

#. box title
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:401
msgid "A trap: numbers as text"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:402
msgid "`max([\"9\", \"10\", \"100\"])` is `\"9\"`: strings compare letter by letter (session 2). Convert first - `key=float` or `float(...)` in the generator."
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:411
msgid "`default=`: when the list can be empty"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:413
msgid "`max([])` is a `ValueError`. When a filter may leave nothing, give a `default`:"
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:422
msgid "`sum`, `len` and the average"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:436
msgid "This is `PROC MEANS` for one variable. The module `statistics` (session 10) adds `median`, `stdev` and more; pandas gives all of them in one `describe()` (session 14)."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:440
msgid "Change the checks block above: is there a loan with `outstanding` greater than `amount`? Is every `stage` one of `\"1\"`, `\"2\"`, `\"3\"`? Predict the answers first."
msgstr ""

#. heading 2
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:444
msgid "3. Sorting, in depth"
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:446
msgid "A recap"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:448
msgid "Session 3: `list.sort()` changes the list, `sorted(x)` returns a new list. Session 4: `key=` sorts by any expression, `reverse=True` sorts from the largest. Today: several columns, mixed directions, and the top N."
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:452
msgid "Several columns: a tuple as the key"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:454
msgid "A key that returns a **tuple** sorts by the first value, then by the second for equal first values, and so on - like `ORDER BY a, b`:"
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:470
msgid "Mixed directions: `ORDER BY product, outstanding DESC`"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:472
msgid "`reverse=True` reverses **all** columns. For mixed directions there are two ways."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:474
msgid "**1. Negate a number.** `-outstanding` sorts the largest first, inside an ascending sort:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:489
msgid "This works only for numbers - you cannot negate a string."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:491
msgid "**2. Two sorts.** Python's sort is **stable**: items with equal keys keep their order. So sort by the **last** column first, then by the first one:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:509
msgid "`PROC SORT` is stable too (with the default `EQUALS` option), so this is familiar."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:513
msgid "For keys that only pick columns, the module `operator` has a ready function: `itemgetter(2)` is the same as `lambda row: row[2]`, and `itemgetter(1, 2)` gives the tuple `(row[1], row[2])`. It is a bit faster and reads like a column list:"
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:530
msgid "The top N"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:532
msgid "`sorted(...)[:n]` - sort, then take a slice (session 3). With `enumerate` (section 4) you get the rank:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:545
msgid "The five clients with the **most** loans - count with a comprehension, sort the pairs:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:559
msgid "(Counting with `list.count` inside a loop reads the list again for every item - slow for big lists. Session 6 counts with a dictionary in one pass.)"
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:562
msgid "A trap: `None` and mixed types"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:564
msgid "`sorted` compares the keys with `<`. Two values that cannot be compared - a number and `None`, a number and a string - stop the sort with a `TypeError`:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:572
msgid "In data, a missing value is common. Decide where it goes, and say it in the key:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:581
msgid "SAS sorts a missing value **first** (it is the smallest number); SQL puts `NULL`s first or last depending on the database. In Python you choose."
msgstr ""

#. box title
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:584
msgid "Advanced: `heapq.nlargest`"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:585
msgid "For the top N of a very long list, the module `heapq` does not sort the whole list:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:595
msgid "It also takes `key=`. For a few thousand rows `sorted(...)[:n]` is just as good."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:599
msgid "In the \"ORDER BY product, outstanding DESC\" example, sort by `product DESC, outstanding DESC` instead. Which of the two ways do you need? (Answer: `reverse=True` on the tuple key is enough - both columns go down.)"
msgstr ""

