#. heading 2
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:749
msgid "5. `map` and `filter`"
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:751
msgid "`map`: one function, every item"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:753
msgid "`map(function, iterable)` calls the function for every item. It is an iterator - wrap it in `list()` to see the values:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:763
msgid "It is most useful with a function that already exists - `float`, `int`, `str.strip`, `str.upper`, your own:"
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:771
msgid "`filter`: keep some items"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:773
msgid "`filter(function, iterable)` keeps the items for which the function returns something true:"
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:785
msgid "`map` / `filter` or a comprehension?"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:787
msgid "Both do the same job:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:799
msgid "Most Python programmers prefer the **comprehension**: it reads like a sentence and needs no `lambda`. `map` is nice when the function already has a name: `map(float, fields)`. You will meet both in other people's code."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:803
msgid "In pandas, `map` comes back: `series.map(function)` or `series.map(dictionary)` - the Python version of a SAS format (sessions 6 and 15)."
msgstr ""

#. box title
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:806
msgid "Advanced: `functools.reduce`"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:807
msgid "`reduce` combines the items into **one** value, two at a time:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:817
msgid "For sums, minimums and maximums the built-in functions are clearer. `reduce` is useful for \"fold\" operations without a built-in - for example, combining many sets with `|`."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:822
msgid "Change `row` in the `map(str.strip, ...)` example: convert the fields to upper case too. Can you do both with one `map`? (Hint: a `lambda`, or a comprehension: `[f.strip().upper() for f in row.split(\",\")]`.)"
