#. heading 2
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1024
msgid "7. Copies of nested lists"
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1026
msgid "A shallow copy shares the rows"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1028
msgid "Session 3: `rows.copy()`, `rows[:]` and `list(rows)` make a **new list**. But the items in it - here the rows - are the **same** objects. The copy is *shallow*:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1042
msgid "The outer list is copied; the inner lists are shared. Changing a **cell** shows in both; adding or removing a **row** does not."
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1045
msgid "`copy.deepcopy`: copy everything"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1047
msgid "`copy.deepcopy` copies the list **and** every list inside it, at every level:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1059
msgid "Use it before you change cells of a table that you still need in its original form - like `data backup; set loans; run;` before an update in SAS."
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1062
msgid "Better: rows that cannot change"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1064
msgid "If the rows are **tuples**, nobody can change a cell - not by mistake, not through a shared reference. To change a value, you build a new row:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1075
msgid "The comprehensions of this lecture always build new rows - `r + [value]`, `(r[0], r[2])` - so they never change the input. That is the safe default for data work."
msgstr ""

#. box title
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1078
msgid "pandas and copies"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1079
msgid "In pandas 3 (and in 2.2 with copy-on-write switched on), a selection behaves like a copy: changing it never changes the original DataFrame. Session 15 shows it."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1083
msgid "**Check yourself.** What does this print?"
msgstr ""

#. box title
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1093 site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1219 site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1277
msgid "Answer"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1094
msgid "`[[1, 2], [7, 4]]`. `b[0] = [9, 9]` puts a new list into `b` only (the outer lists are different). `b[1][0] = 7` changes the inner list that `a` and `b` share."
msgstr ""

