#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1148
msgid "This part is not covered in class. It is for reading at home: two tools that SAS users miss in plain Python - `RETAIN`, `LAG` and BY groups - and a way to read files that do not fit in memory."
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1152
msgid "`itertools`: running totals, `LAG` and BY groups"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1154
msgid "The module `itertools` has building blocks for loops. Three of them replace common DATA step patterns."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1157
msgid "**`accumulate` - a running total (`RETAIN`).** In SAS: `retain total 0; total + x;`."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1167
msgid "**`pairwise` - the value and the one before (`LAG`).** Since Python 3.10:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1177
msgid "It is the same as `zip(xs, xs[1:])` from section 4, without the copy of the list."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1179
msgid "**`groupby` - BY-group processing (`FIRST.` / `LAST.`).** `groupby` cuts a **sorted** list into groups of equal keys - like a DATA step with `BY product`:"
msgstr ""

#. box title
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1196
msgid "Sort first"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1197
msgid "`groupby` starts a new group every time the key **changes**. On unsorted data the same product appears in many small groups - exactly like a SAS `BY` without `PROC SORT`, except that Python does not stop with an error."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1202
msgid "Two more: `islice(iterable, n)` takes the first `n` items of any iterator (`OBS=n`), and `chain(a, b)` reads several lists one after another (`SET a b;`)."
msgstr ""

