#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1234
msgid "Generator functions: `yield`"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1236
msgid "A function with `yield` instead of `return` is a **generator function**. Each call of `next` runs it until the next `yield`, and the function **waits** there, with its local variables, until the next value is asked for:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1250
msgid "This is useful for big files: a generator that reads one row at a time, converts it and gives it on. The whole file is never in memory - only the current row:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:1267
msgid "A generator, like `zip` or `map`, is used up after one pass - here each line calls `loan_rows(...)` again, which opens the file again. A SAS DATA step works this way too: it reads one observation at a time."
msgstr ""

